[4] Wave $1$ reflects from the outer surface of the oil film, while wave $2$ first refracts into the film, reflects from its inner surface, and then emerges;
Figure 198 shows these two paths.
    break: {"before":"The textbook credits the British scientist Thomas Young with an important early explanation of the colors of thin oil films, describing him as the first to reach this ingenious conclusion.\nHis explanation was that wave $1$ and wave $2$ combine where they meet, producing different colors in the reflected light.","after":"Wave $1$ reflects from the outer surface of the oil film, while wave $2$ first refracts into the film, reflects from its inner surface, and then emerges;\nFigure 198 shows these two paths."}
[5] **Light interference is the superposition of two light waves, making the resulting light oscillation stronger or weaker at different points in space.**
At the same point, oscillations that act together produce a stronger combined oscillation, while oscillations that act against each other produce a weaker one, making that point brighter or darker.
