[6] Weaker light does not necessarily mean no light at all;
complete cancellation requires equal wave amplitudes as well as oscillations that exactly oppose each other.
    break: {"before":"**Light interference is the superposition of two light waves, making the resulting light oscillation stronger or weaker at different points in space.**\nAt the same point, oscillations that act together produce a stronger combined oscillation, while oscillations that act against each other produce a weaker one, making that point brighter or darker.","after":"Weaker light does not necessarily mean no light at all;\ncomplete cancellation requires equal wave amplitudes as well as oscillations that exactly oppose each other."}
[7] The film's thickness and the light's wavelength affect the result because they change how far each wave has progressed through its oscillation when the waves meet.
A wave's phase tells us which stage of a complete oscillation cycle it has reached;
    break: {"before":"Weaker light does not necessarily mean no light at all;\ncomplete cancellation requires equal wave amplitudes as well as oscillations that exactly oppose each other.","after":"The film's thickness and the light's wavelength affect the result because they change how far each wave has progressed through its oscillation when the waves meet.\nA wave's phase tells us which stage of a complete oscillation cycle it has reached;"}
[8] comparing the phases where the waves meet tells us whether they reinforce or weaken each other.
Traveling an extra distance delays the oscillation, but the difference in geometric distance is not the whole phase difference, because light travels at different speeds in different media and reflection can also change its phase.
    break: {"before":"The film's thickness and the light's wavelength affect the result because they change how far each wave has progressed through its oscillation when the waves meet.\nA wave's phase tells us which stage of a complete oscillation cycle it has reached;","after":"comparing the phases where the waves meet tells us whether they reinforce or weaken each other.\nTraveling an extra distance delays the oscillation, but the difference in geometric distance is not the whole phase difference, because light travels at different speeds in different media and reflection can also change its phase."}
[9] For light incident nearly straight onto an interface between transparent, nonabsorbing media, reflection from a lower-refractive-index medium toward a higher-index medium reverses the oscillation by half a cycle;
reflection in the opposite direction does not produce this reversal.
