[11] an effective delay of one wavelength represents a phase difference of one complete cycle.
The following rules describe two steady, periodic light waves compared at the same observation point, with the same frequency and aligned oscillation directions, whose phase relationship remains stable throughout the observation.
    break: {"before":"When using the textbook's rules below, the delay must therefore include both the phase delay from propagation and the phase changes from reflection along the two paths, not just the extra distance traveled inside the film.\nExpressing this total phase difference as an equivalent distance, using the wavelength in the same medium at the comparison point, gives the effective delay used here;","after":"an effective delay of one wavelength represents a phase difference of one complete cycle.\nThe following rules describe two steady, periodic light waves compared at the same observation point, with the same frequency and aligned oscillation directions, whose phase relationship remains stable throughout the observation."}
[12] Within this model and total-phase convention, if wave $2$, which enters and returns from the film, has an effective delay relative to reflected wave $1$ equal to an integer number of wavelengths, their oscillations act together and the light is **reinforced**.
If the second wave's effective delay is half a wavelength, or an odd number of half-wavelengths, their oscillations oppose each other and the light is **weakened**.
This is not a geometric-distance rule that can be applied directly to every oil film: first determine whether reflection reverses the phase at each interface, using the media on either side of the film, and then compare the waves' total phases.
