[19] For fixed media and a fixed direction of illumination, the film-thickness conditions for reinforcing or weakening reflected waves depend on their wavelength;
these conditions must still include the phase changes caused by reflection.
    break: {"before":"At an interface, the frequency stays the same, but the propagation speed and wavelength can change, so the medium must be specified when relating wavelength to color.\nWhite light contains many colors, and therefore contains light waves with many different frequencies.","after":"For fixed media and a fixed direction of illumination, the film-thickness conditions for reinforcing or weakening reflected waves depend on their wavelength;\nthese conditions must still include the phase changes caused by reflection."}
[20] A single part of a film can therefore reinforce some reflected colors while weakening others, rather than making every color brighter or darker in the same way.
If the thickness differs from one part of the film to another, the combination of reinforced and weakened colors differs too.
This is why a film of uneven thickness displays different colors under white light.
